VEYLENTRA — WINTER FESTIVAL, SUPER ADMIN FEATURE CONTROL & GAMIFICATION 2.0

PRIMARY OBJECTIVE

Implement a complete Winter Festival experience for Veylentra, together with a reusable Super Admin-controlled feature and theme management system.

This release has five major objectives:

1. Create a beautiful, interactive Winter Theme for Veylentra.
2. Build a Supabase-backed Feature & Theme Control Center inside the Super Admin panel.
3. Restore and substantially improve the Student and Faculty XP Leaderboard.
4. Introduce a special nightly Santa Gift experience with rarity-based rewards.
5. Introduce a recurring Spin Wheel reward system with a two-hour cooldown.

This must be a fully functional implementation—not a collection of decorative frontend components.

Every important setting, reward, claim, cooldown, leaderboard entry, and user-specific progression must be persisted and synchronized through Supabase.

Preserve the existing excellent Student Dashboard and Faculty experience. Do not start the postponed full app-like interface redesign. Make only the targeted UI changes needed for these features.

---

1. SUPER ADMIN FEATURE & THEME MANAGEMENT SYSTEM

This is a foundational requirement.

I want to control newly introduced features, seasonal themes, gamification systems, experiments, and other optional experiences directly from my Super Admin account.

Build a reusable management system inside the existing Super Admin Admin Panel.

Do not implement isolated hardcoded toggles for Winter Theme, Santa, Spin Wheel, or every future feature.

Build a centralized, extensible system.

A. Feature Control Center

Add a clearly organized section inside the Super Admin panel called:

Feature & Theme Management

It should allow Super Admins to:

- View all registered features.
- Enable or disable a feature.
- Enable or disable a theme.
- Enable or disable seasonal events.
- Control gamification modules.
- View each feature's current status.
- View its description.
- View its release information.
- Preview features.
- View when a feature was last updated.
- View who changed its status.
- View rollout information.
- Search and filter the feature registry.

Suggested categories:

- Themes and Appearance.
- Student Features.
- Faculty Features.
- AI Features.
- Gamification.
- Seasonal Events.
- Experimental Features.
- Other Platform Features.

B. Global ON/OFF behavior

The core behavior must be:

When a feature is ON

- It becomes available to all eligible users.
- It appears in the appropriate user interfaces.
- Its functionality becomes accessible.
- Its data and interactions work normally.
- Changes synchronize across the application.

When a feature is OFF

- It becomes available only to authenticated Super Admins.
- Regular students, teachers, coordinators, and other users must not see or access it.
- Super Admins can still access it for previewing, testing, and management.
- The normal user-facing experience must remain unaffected.

This must be enforced by both the frontend and backend.

Important: Hiding a button is not authorization. Disabled features must not remain accessible through direct API requests or manipulated frontend state.

C. Feature registry

Create a centralized feature registry.

Each feature should have a stable unique key and useful metadata.

Suggested fields:

- Feature key.
- Display name.
- Description.
- Category.
- Enabled status.
- Release version.
- Creation timestamp.
- Last updated timestamp.
- Updated by.
- Optional configuration.
- Optional start and end dates.
- Optional theme metadata.

Example feature keys:

- "winter_theme"
- "winter_santa_gifts"
- "winter_spin_wheel"
- "xp_leaderboard"
- "winter_badges"
- "winter_daily_tasks"

These are examples; follow the repository's existing naming conventions.

The registry must be extensible so future developers can register new features without rebuilding the control system.

D. Supabase persistence

Supabase must be the source of truth.

Create or extend the appropriate database structure.

Requirements:

- Persistent feature configuration.
- Secure Super Admin authorization.
- Appropriate Row Level Security policies.
- Server-side permission validation.
- Realtime synchronization where appropriate.
- Safe default behavior when settings cannot be loaded.
- No unauthorized modification.
- Audit history for changes.

When a Super Admin changes a toggle:

1. Validate the Super Admin session on the server.
2. Validate the requested feature key.
3. Persist the new state in Supabase.
4. Return the authoritative updated state.
5. Synchronize the state across connected application sessions.
6. Update the UI without requiring users to manually refresh unnecessarily.

If a feature-state request fails, do not pretend the toggle succeeded.

E. Feature access hook/service

Create a reusable application-level feature access service.

For example, conceptually:

- Check whether a feature is enabled.
- Check whether the current user is an authorized Super Admin.
- Determine whether the feature should be visible.
- Determine whether its actions are permitted.
- Subscribe to feature-state changes.

Follow existing project conventions rather than introducing an unnecessary new framework.

All feature-gated components should use the same centralized mechanism.

Avoid inconsistent checks scattered throughout the application.

F. Safety and failure behavior

If the feature configuration cannot be loaded:

- Do not accidentally expose disabled features.
- Do not grant unauthorized access.
- Preserve the existing application functionality.
- Display a reasonable error or retry state for Super Admins.

Feature toggles must not disable critical authentication, security, data integrity, or other essential platform infrastructure.

Do not make the application dependent on an optional theme or seasonal feature to function.

